---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';

// Excerpts are trimmed for readability and redacted: the real spreadsheet ID,
// the credential handling and the deployment URL are not published here.

const balanceFormula = `// A member's hours balance is a formula written into the sheet at signup,
// not a number stored and updated by hand. Hours given minus hours received,
// counting only rows that both sides have verified.
sheet.getRange(newRow, 21).setFormula(
  '=IF(A' + newRow + '="", 0, ' +
    'SUMIFS(Transactions!J:J, Transactions!D:D, A' + newRow + ', Transactions!L:L, "Yes") - ' +
    'SUMIFS(Transactions!J:J, Transactions!G:G, A' + newRow + ', Transactions!L:L, "Yes"))'
);`;

const guardianAuth = `// A match can be accepted by the member who made the request, or by that
// member's guardian. Anyone else is refused.
var isAuth = (reqMemberId === String(me['MemberID']));

if (!isAuth) {
  var child = members.filter(function (m) {
    return String(m['MemberID'])   === reqMemberId &&
           String(m['GuardianID']) === String(me['MemberID']);
  });
  if (child.length > 0) isAuth = true;
}

if (!isAuth) {
  return { success: false, error: 'Only the requester or their guardian can accept a match.' };
}`;

const dupeGuard = `// One set of hours per job per helper. A rejected submission can be corrected
// and resent, so rejected rows are excluded from the duplicate check.
var dupe = existing.filter(function (t) {
  return String(t['RequestID']) === String(data.requestId) &&
         String(t['GiverID'])   === String(giverId) &&
         t['TransactionVerified?'] !== 'Rejected';
});

if (dupe.length > 0) {
  return { success: false, error: 'Hours have already been logged for this job.' };
}`;
---

<Layout
  title="ACC Timebank | Joe Fazio"
  description="Case study: a time-banking system built for a Florida mutual-aid group on Google Apps Script and Google Sheets."
>
  <p class="back"><a href="/projects">Back to projects</a></p>

  <h1>ACC Timebank</h1>
  <p class="meta">CIS 484 capstone · Spring 2026 · lead developer</p>

  <dl class="spec">
    <div>
      <dt>Client</dt>
      <dd>Alachua Community Collective, a mutual-aid group in Florida</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Role</dt>
      <dd>Lead developer on the quick hit track, requirements gathering across both tracks</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Built with</dt>
      <dd>Google Apps Script, Google Sheets, Python</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Status</dt>
      <dd>Capstone complete, no longer operated by ACC</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <h2>The problem</h2>

  <p>
    ACC runs a time bank. Members trade hours of work rather than money: an hour
    of yard care earns an hour of somebody else's help later. Someone has to keep
    that ledger, and keeping it by hand stops working as soon as the group grows.
    ACC asked for two tracks, a fast low-cost version built on tools they already
    had and a fuller custom system. I led the first and did most of the
    requirements gathering across the whole engagement.
  </p>

  <h2>Constraints</h2>

  <p>
    The constraints shaped this build more than the technology did. Five
    mattered:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>No budget. Anything with a monthly bill was out.</li>
    <li>Administrators were volunteers, not technical staff.</li>
    <li>Member intake already ran through a Google Form, and members were used to it.</li>
    <li>Minors participate, which means guardian consent and guardian oversight.</li>
    <li>Hours are the currency, so a wrong balance is a real dispute between neighbors.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why not AppSheet</h2>

  <p>
    I started in AppSheet and moved off it. It handled forms and lists well, but
    the ledger needed conditional multi-step logic: one action writing to several
    tables, a verification step performed by a second party, and a balance
    derived from rows rather than stored. Expressing that in AppSheet meant
    fighting the tool on every change. Abandoning it was the right call and it
    was mine to make.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a Google Sheet as the database</h2>

  <p>
    Intake already ran through a Google Form, and a Form writes to a Sheet.
    Building on that Sheet meant no data migration, no new accounts, no new tool
    for members, and administrators who could inspect the raw data in something
    they already understood. A real database would have been technically cleaner
    and would have been the wrong recommendation for this client.
  </p>

  <h2>How the ledger works</h2>

  <p>
    A balance is never stored. It is a formula written into each member's row at
    signup, summing verified hours given and subtracting verified hours received.
    Nothing can drift out of sync, because there is no second copy of the number
    to drift.
  </p>

  <Code code={balanceFormula} lang="js" theme="github-dark" />

  <p>
    The <code>"Yes"</code> condition is the important part. Hours count only once
    the receiving side has confirmed them, so nobody can credit themselves by
    filing a transaction alone.
  </p>

  <h2>The request lifecycle</h2>

  <p>
    Every job moves through five states, and each transition needs an action from
    a specific person:
  </p>

  <ol class="states">
    <li><strong>Open.</strong> Posted, waiting for a volunteer.</li>
    <li><strong>PendingMatch.</strong> A volunteer claimed it, the requester has not agreed yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Matched.</strong> The requester accepted, and both sides now see contact details.</li>
    <li><strong>PendingVerification.</strong> The helper logged hours, the requester has not confirmed.</li>
    <li><strong>Completed.</strong> Confirmed, and the hours count toward both balances.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Contact details are withheld until <strong>Matched</strong> on purpose.
    Requesters can decline a volunteer before any personal information changes
    hands, which came directly out of requirements gathering with ACC.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hours can be submitted once per job per helper, and a rejected submission can
    be corrected and resent:
  </p>

  <Code code={dupeGuard} lang="js" theme="github-dark" />

  <h2>Youth accounts and guardians</h2>

  <p>
    Minors participate in the time bank, so a youth member has a guardian linked
    to their record and no login of their own. Guardians claim jobs on a child's
    behalf, accept matches for them, and receive the contact details in place of
    the child. Every action on a youth record checks that relationship first.
  </p>

  <Code code={guardianAuth} lang="js" theme="github-dark" />

  <p>
    This was not in the original brief. It came out of asking who actually uses
    the system, which is the part of this project I would point to first.
  </p>

  <h2>The rest of the build</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Admin reporting</strong> for verifying members, resolving disputed
      transactions and voiding bad rows without deleting history.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A burnout-risk flag</strong> surfacing members who give far more
      than they receive, and members who hoard hours without spending them. Both
      patterns predict someone quietly dropping out, so an administrator gets
      told to check in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A Python migration script</strong> that moved existing Google Form
      responses into the new schema, so the system launched with the real
      membership already in it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A skills taxonomy</strong> of eighteen services across six
      categories, so requests could be matched to members offering that skill.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What I would do differently</h2>

  <p>
    Two things, and the first is a security problem I can identify now and could
    not identify then.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Passwords are stored in plaintext.</strong> There is a SHA-256
    hashing function in the code, but registration writes the raw password to the
    sheet and the login check accepts either value, so the plaintext comparison
    is the one that matches. Anyone who can open the spreadsheet can read every
    member's password. The correct approach is a slow hash designed for
    passwords, bcrypt or Argon2, with a unique random salt per user rather than
    one shared constant, and never writing the raw value at all. Apps Script
    makes that awkward, which is an argument for handing authentication to Google
    sign-in rather than building it, not an argument for storing plaintext.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Business logic is split between the sheet and the script.</strong>
    Balances live in spreadsheet formulas while every other rule lives in Apps
    Script. That was a deliberate trade for administrator visibility and I would
    make it again for this client, but it means the rules sit in two places and a
    reader has to know both to follow them.
  </p>

  <h2>Status</h2>

  <p>
    The capstone ended and ACC no longer runs the system. The deployment still
    exists behind a Google login, so there is no public demo, and there is no
    public repository. The excerpts above are redacted: the spreadsheet
    identifier, the credential handling and the deployment URL are not reproduced
    here.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second track, a fuller system on ASP.NET Core and Azure SQL, was built by
    a teammate. My contact with it was confirming that CSV exports from my side
    imported cleanly into theirs. They did.
  </p>

  <p class="back"><a href="/projects">Back to projects</a></p>
</Layout>

